From davidm@AZStarNet.com Sat Jun 15 16:33:59 1996
Message-ID: <199606152333.QAA05589@koala.azstarnet.com>
Date: Sat, 15 Jun 1996 16:33:59 -0700
From: David Mosberger-Tang <davidm@AZStarNet.com>
Subject: Re: performance / f2c bug

>>>>> On Sat, 15 Jun 1996 18:50:52 +0200 (MET DST), koenig@tat.physik.uni-tuebingen.de (Harald Koenig) said:

  >> Did you use "cc -std1"?  Otherwise, you get tons of float->double
  >> and vice versa converstions.

  Harald> of course I did not.  I just browsed the man page for
  Harald> "-std1" but couldn't find any pointer to this
  Harald> behaviour. anyway, this makes a big differece giving the
  Harald> same performace with DEC-cc (using f2c) now like with
  Harald> DEC-f77:

  Harald> where is this switch for gcc?

gcc has the inverse switch: -traditional.  gcc by default operates in
ANSI mode, cc in K&R mode.  Back in the old days (umh, is it really
_that_ long?? ;-), C folks where to lazy to declare prototypes.  As a
result, the compilers had to be conservative and always pass arguments
in the widest possible form (floats as doubles, char, short, and int
as long).  Similarly, all expression evaluation was done in the widest
type (long & double, again).  In other words, "float" used to make
sense only for the purpose of saving memory.

Maybe DEC can be convinced that the time has come to make -std1 the
default?

In case of doubt, objdump -S will tell it all... ;-)

	--david

